**Read:** Finish the family-specific readings, graduate mechanics work and all three advanced studies. Return to the shared quantitative, chemistry, metrology, mineralogy, metallurgy and mechanics Records where a derivation or measurement remains unclear.

Explain how bonding, structure, processing and environment connect to elasticity, time response, plasticity, fracture, fatigue and creep. Compare metals, ceramics/glasses, polymers/elastomers, composites and natural/cellular materials with actual state and test definitions. Derive the linear hereditary energy balance, a strengthening law, crack driving/resistance, weakest-link scaling and laminate force/moment relations.

**Demonstrate:** Independently reproduce the original loading-history calculation and its energy account; defend the three studies with full source methods and observations; compare plausible alternatives; quantify uncertainty and domain limits; and revise an interpretation after criticism. A successful review requires explanations and evidence, not simply visiting every Record.

Continue into functional properties, connecting structure and material history with charge, heat, polarization, light and magnetism. Fabrication, degradation, selection and service build on these mechanical and functional accounts.

